  "worked_solution": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the population mean $\\mu =12,$ the population standard deviation $\\sigma =0.4,$ and that the sample size $n=100.$ Therefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(12,\\frac{{0.4}^{2}}{100}) \\\\  & ∼N(12,0.0016).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>12.1).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>12.1) & =P(Z>\\frac{12.1-12}{\\sqrt{0.0016}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>2.5) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<2.5) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(2.5)=0.9938.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>12.1) & =1-Φ(2.5) \\\\  & =1-0.9938 \\\\  & =0.0062.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
